:::
:::


Early on the proportion lurches — up near 0.8, down towards 0.3 — and by a few hundred flips it has calmed down and sits close to one half. One thing to notice about the horizontal axis: it is on a log scale, so the flips are not evenly spaced. Equal distances along it mean the flip count has *multiplied* (1 to 10 to 100 to 1000), which spreads out the early flips where the proportion is wildest and squeezes the later ones together. None of the four runs lands exactly on 0.5, and none ever would in a finite number of flips, but the pull towards it is unmistakable. Hold on to this picture. A quantity that is wild for small samples and steadies as the sample grows is the seed of the whole session, and you'll meet it again in the room, built from real measurements instead of coin flips.

Better than reading it off a fixed picture is generating it yourself. The panel below flips a fresh fair coin the number of times you choose and traces the running proportion. Flip it again and again: the early wildness and the eventual settling show up every time, but the *particular* path is different on every run, which is the whole point — the settling is reliable, the individual wobble is not.

